            0.116,
            0.426,
            0.881,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, the curved surface area of the cylinder is the area of the rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.471,
            0.703,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• The curved surface area (CSA) of the cylinder is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.298,
            0.495,
            0.698,
            0.515
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{CSA}=\\mathrm{circun~free~nc~} \\times \\mathrm{height}=2 \\pi r h."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.521,
            0.688,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If the cylinder is closed at both ends (top and bottom) like a biscuit tin (see Fig. 14.5B), then the flat circular surfaces at the ends also contribute to the area. Since there are two ends,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.602,
            0.601,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "total surface area "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(TSA) = 2\\pi r h + 2\\pi r^{2} = 2\\pi r(h + r)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.708,
            0.522,
            0.895,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.708,
                0.522,
                0.895,
                0.642
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_14.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.652,
            0.685,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If the cylinder is closed at one end and open at the other end, like a tumbler, then"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.753,
            0.658,
            0.851,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 14.5B"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.298,
            0.696,
            0.698,
            0.715
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_5_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "{ \\mathrm { s u r f a } } { \\overline { { \\mathrm { c e } } } } { \\mathrm { a r e a } } = 2 \\pi r h + \\pi r ^ { 2 } = \\pi r ( 2 h + r ) ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.724,
            0.53,
            0.744
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The volume V of the cylinder is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.287,
            0.749,
            0.708,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "V = cross-sectional area × height = πr"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "h."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.781,
            0.7,
            0.824
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "14.2.1 Another Way of Understanding Why the Above Formulas Are True"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.835,
            0.745,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To understand why the formulas for the surface area (curved part) and the volume of a cylinder hold, it helps to think of the cylinder as a large number of thin circular khakhras stacked on one another."
            }
          ]
        },
